{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "tr-03-multiple-crossing-time-inference",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Finding Train Length and Speed from Multiple Crossing Times",
    "description": "Use two observations of the same train to eliminate unknowns and recover its speed, length, or another object's length.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach elimination through crossing-time differences: pole vs platform, two platforms, stationary trains, equal-length shortcuts, then a three-observation capstone. The learner sees why subtraction removes the repeated train length.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 20, Problems on Trains. Approved scope: Teach inference from repeated crossings: pole time plus platform time, two platform lengths with two times, a pole plus a stationary train, and equal-length stationary-train cases. Emphasize subtraction before algebra: the train length appears in both observations, so subtracting the distances often removes it instantly. Then recover the train length from speed × pole time. Include capstones that combine a pole, a platform, and a stationary train in one chain."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The fastest train solvers notice what stays the same.\n\nWhen one train crosses two different objects, its own length and speed are unchanged. Subtracting the observations often makes the hidden part disappear.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When the same train gives two crossing times, do not rush into two equations. First ask what changed.\n\nIf pole time is 9 s and an 84 m platform takes 21 s, the only new distance is 84 m and the only new time is 12 s.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So speed = 84/12 = 7 m/s = 25.2 km/hr.\nThen train length = 7 × 9 = 63 m.\n\nSubtraction removes the hidden train length before you ever name it.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
